\section{\code{mode = "reference"}}{

The metadata column (default \code{"reference"}) gives, for each sample, the ID
of the sample it should be compared with. IDs must match sample names in
the count table. Samples with \code{NA} (for example the reference samples
themselves) are not compared. Typical uses:
\itemize{
\item FMT: each recipient sample's reference is its donor's sample.
\item Longitudinal: each sample's reference is the same subject's
baseline sample.
\item A single reference for everyone, such as one donor or a mock
community: every row gets the same ID.
}

Each sample is compared with its own reference (\code{comparison = "own"}) and
with every other reference sample in the column (\code{comparison = "other"}).
The \code{other} distances are a natural baseline: in FMT, a recipient who is
closer to their own donor than to other donors shows donor-specific
change rather than a general shift. Filter to \code{comparison == "own"} if you
only need the own-reference distances.
}

\section{\code{mode = "group"}}{

The metadata column (default \code{"group"}) defines groups. Every pair of
samples is returned once and labelled \code{"within"} (same group) or
\code{"between"} (different groups). Every metadata column is included for
both samples (\verb{<name>_1}, \verb{<name>_2}), so pairs are easy to filter, e.g.
\code{res[res$role_1 != res$role_2, ]} for mother-infant pairs only.
}

\section{Statistical testing}{

akeR does not test the distances, because the appropriate test depends on
the study design. Note that pairwise distances are not independent: each
sample appears in many pairs. A Wilcoxon or t-test on the rows of the
output (as done by \code{ggpubr::stat_compare_means()}) treats them as
independent and gives p-values that are too small. Consider permutation
tests that shuffle labels across samples, PERMANOVA
(\code{\link[vegan:adonis2]{vegan::adonis2()}}), or models with random effects for samples or
subjects.
